.TH ORO-LATICA-NAT 3 "" "Oro Runtime" "Oro Runtime API Manual"
.SH NAME
oro:latica/nat \- JavaScript API module
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
import * as module from 'oro:latica/nat'
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
oro:latica/nat is a JavaScript API module exposed by the Oro Runtime.
.SH REFERENCE
.TP
\fBMAPPING_ENDPOINT_INDEPENDENT\fR
The NAT type is encoded using 5 bits:
.br
0b00001 : the lsb indicates if endpoint dependence information is included 0b00010 : the second bit indicates the endpoint dependence value
.br
0b00100 : the third bit indicates if firewall information is included 0b01000 : the fourth bit describes which requests can pass the firewall, only known IPs (0) or any IP (1) 0b10000 : the fifth bit describes which requests can pass the firewall, only known ports (0) or any port (1) Every remote will see the same IP:PORT mapping for this peer.
.br
:3333 ┌──────┐ :1111 ┌───▶ │ R1 │ ┌──────┐ ┌───────┐ │ └──────┘ │ P1 ├───▶│ NAT ├──┤ └──────┘ └───────┘ │ ┌──────┐ └───▶ │ R2 │ :3333 └──────┘
.TP
\fBMAPPING_ENDPOINT_DEPENDENT\fR
Every remote will see a different IP:PORT mapping for this peer.
.br
:4444 ┌──────┐ :1111 ┌───▶ │ R1 │ ┌──────┐ ┌───────┐ │ └──────┘ │ P1 ├───▶│ NAT ├──┤ └──────┘ └───────┘ │ ┌──────┐ └───▶ │ R2 │ :5555 └──────┘
.TP
\fBFIREWALL_ALLOW_ANY\fR
The firewall allows the port mapping to be accessed by: \- Any IP:PORT combination (FIREWALL_ALLOW_ANY) \- Any PORT on a previously connected IP (FIREWALL_ALLOW_KNOWN_IP) \- Only from previously connected IP:PORT combinations (FIREWALL_ALLOW_KNOWN_IP_AND_PORT)
.TP
\fBFIREWALL_ALLOW_KNOWN_IP\fR
This is an exported `VariableDeclaration` named `FIREWALL_ALLOW_KNOWN_IP`, but it does not yet have a dedicated description in the generated reference.
.TP
\fBFIREWALL_ALLOW_KNOWN_IP_AND_PORT\fR
This is an exported `VariableDeclaration` named `FIREWALL_ALLOW_KNOWN_IP_AND_PORT`, but it does not yet have a dedicated description in the generated reference.
.TP
\fBUNKNOWN\fR
The initial state of the nat is unknown and its value is 0
.TP
\fBUNRESTRICTED\fR
Full\-cone NAT, also known as one\-to\-one NAT
.br
Any external host can send packets to iAddr:iPort by sending packets to eAddr:ePort.
.TP
\fBADDR_RESTRICTED\fR
(Address)\-restricted\-cone NAT
.br
An external host (hAddr:any) can send packets to iAddr:iPort by sending packets to eAddr:ePort only if iAddr:iPort has previously sent a packet to hAddr:any. "Any" means the port number doesn't matter.
.TP
\fBPORT_RESTRICTED\fR
Port\-restricted cone NAT
.br
An external host (hAddr:hPort) can send packets to iAddr:iPort by sending packets to eAddr:ePort only if iAddr:iPort has previously sent a packet to hAddr:hPort.
.br
has previously messaged you from this *specific* port.
.TP
\fBENDPOINT_RESTRICTED\fR
Symmetric NAT
.br
Only an external host that receives a packet from an internal host can send a packet back.
.br
by itself, the mapping it created is only valid for you.
.TP
\fBisEndpointDependenceDefined\fR
Returns true iff a valid MAPPING_ENDPOINT_* flag has been added (indicated by the lsb).
.TP
\fBisFirewallDefined\fR
Returns true iff a valid FIREWALL_ALLOW_* flag has been added (indicated by the third bit).
.TP
\fBisValid\fR
Returns true iff both FIREWALL_ALLOW_* and MAPPING_ENDPOINT_* flags have been added.
.TP
\fBtoString\fR
toString returns a human\-readable label for the NAT enum
.TP
\fBtoStringStrategy\fR
toStringStrategy returns a human\-readable label for the STRATEGY enum
